        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.612,
            0.069,
            0.815,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "The World of Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.069,
            0.883,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "61"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.105,
            0.88,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "| General repeating | • Let x = the decimal number. • Multiply by 10m where m = the number of non-repeating digits, then multiply by 10n where n = the number of repeating digits. • Subtract from the previous equation, and solve for x. |\n| --- | --- |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_table_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.245,
            0.558,
            0.268
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.6.2 The Magic of Cyclic Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.28,
            0.886,
            0.369
          ],
          "content": "When we calculate the decimal for $\\frac { 1 } { 7 }$ we get 0.142857142857 … or 0.142857. The repeating block, 142857, is one of mathematics’ most fascinating gem —  a **cyclic number**. Watch what happens when we multiply it by the digits 1 through 6:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.373,
            0.63,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "1 4 2 8 5 7 \\times 1 = 1 4 2 8 5 7",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.399,
            0.629,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "142857 \\times 2 = 285714",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.426,
            0.627,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": "142857 \\times 3 = 428571",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.452,
            0.629,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "142857 \\times 4 = 571428",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.478,
            0.629,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "142857 \\times 5 = 714285",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.506,
            0.63,
            0.527
          ],
          "content": "1 4 2 8 5 7 \\times 6 = 8 5 7 1 4 2",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.537,
            0.885,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": "The same digits simply shift in a cyclic circle! This beautiful internal structure is a hallmark of the rational number $\\frac { 1 } { 7 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.614,
            0.679,
            0.637
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.6.3 Irrational Decimals: Chaos and Infinity"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.643,
            0.885,
            0.703
          ],
          "content": "Irrational numbers, however, possess decimal expansions that never end and never repeat. There is no cyclic block, no pattern that loops forever. Examples include:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.35,
            0.711,
            0.694,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": "2 = 1.4142135623730950488 …"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.742,
            0.693,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "π = 3.1415926535897932384 …"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.797,
            0.622,
            0.825
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 3.5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.844,
            0.886,
            0.921
          ],
          "content": "1. Without performing long division, determine which of the following rational numbers will have terminating decimals and which will be repeating: $\\frac { 7 } { 2 0 } , \\frac { 4 } { 1 5 }$ and $\\frac { 1 3 } { 2 5 0 }$ . Then check your answers"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
